# Cloudflare Pages reads this from the published root and does not serve it.
# Quarto skips `_`-prefixed files, so `_quarto.yml` names it under `resources:`
# explicitly; without that line the file is dropped from `docs/` in silence and
# every rule below simply never exists.
#
# Two tiers, and the split is the whole point.
#
# The fonts never change. A face is a pinned upstream release, and when one is
# replaced it goes out under a NEW FILENAME rather than over the old bytes --
# which is what earns `immutable` on paths that carry no content hash.
# `immutable` means a browser will not revalidate even on a reload, so a file
# replaced in place would be stuck for a year with no way to bust it. Renaming
# is the escape hatch, and it costs nothing: the stylesheet is what names the
# file, and the stylesheet goes stale in an hour.
#
# So the stylesheets are the lever, and they stay short. They are ~3 KB against
# ~2 MB of faces, so nothing is saved by caching them hard, and everything a
# consumer can be told later -- a new face, a renamed file, a dropped family --
# has to travel through them.
#
# Every rule matches by extension, and never `/assets/*`, because two matching
# rules do not resolve in favour of the specific one: Pages CONCATENATES their
# values into a single field. A `/assets/*` year beside a `/assets/*.css` hour
# was served as
#
#   cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable, public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate
#
# which is two max-ages and an `immutable` on a file that must stay bustable.
# Duplicate directives have no dependable resolution across caches, and
# Chromium takes the first, so the stylesheets would have quietly inherited the
# year. Matching disjointly is what makes each file's lifetime the one written
# down here.
#
# Anything without a rule keeps the Pages default, which is
# `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` -- revalidated every time, never stale.
# That is why the licence .txt files are absent rather than listed: they are
# tiny, rarely fetched, and must not be stuck for a year. It is also what makes
# a new asset type fail safe -- too short, never unbustable.
#
# One number below is not honoured on font.kolen.dev, and the difference is not
# in this repository. Cloudflare serves whichever is higher, the origin
# `max-age` or the zone's Browser Cache TTL, and that zone is on Cloudflare's
# four-hour default. The year survives, being higher. The hour is raised to
# four. It stays an hour here because it is the right number, because a fork
# deploying anywhere else gets it, and because the fix is one zone setting --
# Caching -> Configuration -> Browser Cache TTL -> Respect Existing Headers --
# rather than a worse value written down to match a setting.
#
# Measured rather than assumed, though not end to end: font.kolen.dev returns
# max-age=14400 for a .css whose Pages origin sends max-age=0, which is the
# zone raising it, while .txt passes through at max-age=0 because it is not a
# default-cached extension. The hour's fate follows from the same rule and
# cannot be observed directly until this reaches production.

/assets/*.woff2
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/assets/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate
